{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_gain",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "The target mapping is dL_dt = interface_mobility*interfacial_tension*(1/L - L/L_max**2), so at fixed interface_mobility it is a single curvature-driven gain times one finite-size suppression factor.",
      "child_relation": "Replace the ideal linear finite-size suppression with a sharper crowding/screening dependence on L/L_max, so the target remains curvature-driven at small L but bends down more strongly as L approaches the accessible ceiling.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "L",
        "L_max",
        "interface_mobility"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "At matched early-time mobility and small-L behavior, the child shows a flatter late-time tail and earlier rollover of dL_dt versus L/L_max than the parent, while preserving nonnegative growth."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "reduced_size",
        "expression": "L/L_max",
        "role": "Distance to the finite-size ceiling controlling the onset of late-stage suppression"
      },
      {
        "name": "curvature_drive",
        "expression": "interfacial_tension*interface_mobility/L",
        "role": "Unsuppressed Allen-Cahn-type curvature-driven coarsening scale"
      },
      {
        "name": "ceiling_proximity",
        "expression": "1 - L**2/L_max**2",
        "role": "Parent linear-in-area finite-size availability factor whose idealized form is being relaxed"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when trajectories explore an appreciable fraction of the available scale, i.e. L/L_max is no longer small and especially in the late coarsening window before saturation; weak when L << L_max throughout observation.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, both models agree on the early 1/L regime, but the refined model already shows stronger downward curvature in dL_dt as L/L_max increases. In holdout with longer observation windows or smaller effective L_max, that same mechanism produces a more pronounced late-time slowdown.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law in the limit that the generalized finite-size suppression reduces back to the current linear form in L/L_max, or observationally when L/L_max remains asymptotically small over the full window.",
    "freedom_change": "add_state",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "A sharper finite-size suppression requires one additional latent microstructural occupancy/crowding variable to encode how much coarsening capacity remains beyond what instantaneous L alone captures; existing states cannot change the target mapping except through the current single-gain mobility factor.",
    "gap_statement": "The current model assumes finite-size limitation enters through a fixed linear availability factor in L^2/L_max^2, which is too idealized for late-stage impingement and topology loss of mobile interfaces.",
    "baseline_assumption": "Finite-size saturation is represented by a simple mean-field linear suppression factor multiplying the curvature drive.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "As domains become comparable to the remaining microstructural span, interface segments become topologically screened and the active fraction of curvature-responsive interface declines nonlinearly, producing stronger late-stage slowdown than the parent predicts.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "L",
      "L_max",
      "interface_mobility"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the parent's single-gain finite-size assumption rather than add an unrelated process. Vary the accessible ceiling relative to the evolving domain size, equivalently observing conditions that reach larger L/L_max, while holding interfacial_tension fixed and keeping the same mobility-aging law. The parent predicts one specific rollover of dL_dt with L: after factoring out interface_mobility*interfacial_tension, the suppression is exactly linear in L/L_max through 1/L - L/L_max**2. A child with nonlinear loss of active interface occupancy changes the scored target mapping itself, not just auxiliary trajectories: at the same L and interface_mobility, dL_dt drops more steeply near saturation while matching the parent at small L/L_max. This contrast is scientifically admissible in finite systems or limited remaining microstructural bandwidth where interfaces impinge and mobile curvature sources become depleted. The effect is weak if observations remain in the asymptotic early regime L << L_max, so the refinement should be judged only where late-stage approach to the ceiling is actually sampled. A single refit of the parent parameters cannot generally reproduce both the same early-time small-L curvature-driven rate and the stronger late-time flattening across conditions with different explored L/L_max ranges, because its target dependence on L is locked to one fixed functional form once interface_mobility(t) is specified."
  },
